# Low-weight modular and cusp-form seeds at level four

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.exists_weighted_cusp_seeds_level_four`
(`9af94959-aa50-47b9-85f6-e2ab387a3d8e`), by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08.

Proof: submission `484ba9db-8526-4c56-a189-0d5c28abbd1b`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

There exist forms $A\in M_1(\Gamma_1(4))$, $B\in M_2(\Gamma_1(4))$ and a nonzero cusp form $D\in
S_5(\Gamma_1(4))$ such that their q-expansions at infinity satisfy

$$\operatorname{ord}_q(A)=0,\qquad\operatorname{ord}_q(B)=1.$$

One choice is $A(z)=\eta(2z)^{10}/(\eta(z)^4\eta(4z)^4)$, $B(z)=\eta(4z)^8/\eta(2z)^4$, and
$D(z)=\eta(z)^4\eta(2z)^2\eta(4z)^4$. Their weighted monomials supply the MTT odd-weight level-four
cusp-form dimension lower bound. The odd-weight forms are asserted on $\Gamma_1(4)$, not with
trivial character on $\Gamma_0(4)$.

## Explanation of the source proof

The nonzero weight-five cusp form is now supplied by a separately proved eta-product construction.
Its transformation law is proved on the two generators of Γ₁(4), and cuspidality follows from its
square, a known weight-ten cusp form.

The remaining input is precisely the [weight-one and weight-two modular
pair](p2m:theorem/8fc01707-7844-4954-9f1f-3a25f2514adc) with q-orders zero and one. Combining that
pair with the [weight-five cusp form](p2m:theorem/eaa40a7c-7390-4345-af71-9e5fcc0cf0f7) gives the
three required seeds. No property of the two modular seeds is hidden in the cusp-form construction.
The reduction and its exact frozen target-type comparison compile locally at default limits.

